Chapter 28 — so tonight?
The club had emptied. Lights were down to closing levels. Most of the staff had already clocked out. Curtis was wiping the last of the bar when Janice’s voice carried from the hallway.
“Beardy. In my office.”
He set the rag down and headed back. Hina was still by the staff lockers, apron off, watching him go. She didn’t say anything. Just met his eyes for a second before he turned the corner.
Janice’s office was small. Desk, two chairs, the night’s numbers still up on the monitor. She was standing, arms crossed, when he stepped in. The door clicked shut behind him.
“Sit.”
He did. She stayed on her feet.
“Lip stayed closed. Good. You followed the bar order. Also good.” She studied him. “That doesn’t fix the pattern. Two nights. Two marks. Forest training that leaves you looking like you went ten rounds with something that doesn’t care about customer-facing jobs. I need to know this isn’t going to be every shift between now and your Festival.”
Curtis met her eyes, expression even. “I can keep the visible stuff off my face. That’s the part that matters for the floor.”
“It’s not just the face.” Janice’s tone stayed firm, not loud. “It’s you treating the hours you work here like they’re optional recovery time. Tatsuya and I built this place so the people on the floor are safe and respected. That includes you. If you walk in unable to do the job because you spent the night getting torn up in the woods, I will cut the shifts. Not as punishment. As management.” She paused. “Are we clear?”
“Clear.”
She nodded once. Some of the edge eased. “Ice is still in the well if you need it on the way out. And Beardy—whatever you’re training for, it can survive you taking one night off before you open the lip again. Think about that.”
“I will.” He stood. “Thanks for not sending me home.”
“Don’t make me regret it.” She waved him toward the door. “Get out of here. See you tomorrow if the face cooperates.”
He left. Hina was still by the lockers when he came out. She fell in beside him without asking. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while they headed for the side door and the city night waited outside.
Hina was waiting outside the side door, bag on her shoulder, jacket already on. The street was quiet. Club lights were off behind them. She looked up when he stepped out.
Curtis paused. “Hina? Were you waiting for me?”
“Yeah.” She fell in beside him as he started walking. “Figured Janice would keep you a minute. And I didn’t feel like taking the train alone after she spent the night glaring at your lip.” She glanced at him. “How bad was it?”
“Standard.” He adjusted his jacket. “Bar support until the cut fades. Don’t show up worse tomorrow. She meant it.”
Hina nodded. They walked under the streetlights, close enough that their arms brushed. The usual warmth sat under her worry. “You gonna actually rest tonight? Or are you disappearing into the trees again the second I look away?”
“Rest.” He said it simply. “At least until morning. Forest can wait.”
She didn’t fully believe him. She stayed next to him anyway. “Yui’s probably still up. If you come upstairs she won’t let you leave until you’ve eaten something that isn’t bar nuts.”
“Might take her up on that.” The corner of his mouth twitched, careful. “You two don’t have to keep checking on me.”
“We know.” Hina’s shoulder bumped his. “We’re doing it anyway.”
They kept walking toward the station. The city moved around them. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while the night settled and the conversation didn’t need to fill every step.
They were a block from the station when Hina spoke again, quieter.
“Hey. That guy that was harassing me and stabbed you. He was found dead this morning.”
Curtis shrugged, hands in his pockets. “So?”
She glanced at him, then at the sidewalk. “That’s not the craziest part. The man was gross. He had ongoing cases I don’t want to repeat. I don’t believe in that sort of thing, but… maybe it was best he didn’t live. That’s cruel. I don’t mean that.”
Curtis stopped. He pulled one hand free and held hers, scarred palm warm against her fingers. His voice stayed even.
“It’s not wrong to think that way about people who consistently do harm to others and laugh about it.”
Hina’s fingers tightened around his. She didn’t pull away. The flush was there, but it sat under something heavier. “You really think so?”
“Yeah.” He started walking again, still holding her hand. “The system had him. More than once. He kept going. Someone finished it. That’s the part that matters. Not whether the thought feels clean.”
She walked closer. Their shoulders brushed. She didn’t ask who had finished it. He didn’t offer. The station lights grew brighter ahead of them while the low, steady pull in his chest remained quiet and her hand stayed in his the rest of the way.
They reached the building. Yui was on the balcony again, leaning on the rail in an oversized shirt, when she spotted them coming up the walk. Her grin was immediate.
“Oh hey there, holding hands? Hina, bring that fine piece of ass up here.”
Curtis tilted his head, heterochromia catching the entrance light. “Fine piece of what?”
Hina covered a laugh with her free hand, cheeks pink. Yui cackled. “You. Get up here. Both of you. I made too much food and I’m not eating it alone while you two do the slow-walk romantic thing on the sidewalk.”
Curtis glanced at Hina, still holding her hand. “She always like this?”
“Only when she’s awake.” Hina tugged him toward the door. “Come on. If we don’t go up she’ll just come down and drag you.”
They headed inside. Yui disappeared from the balcony to meet them at the apartment door, still grinning, while the night air followed them up the stairs and the low, steady pull in Curtis’s chest stayed quiet under the ordinary noise of coming home.
Yui shut the apartment door behind them and leaned against it, grin wide. “So. How did you like that photo from us last night?”
Curtis smirked. He pulled his phone out and gave it a small wave. “Saved. Did you guys know you could hide photos on the iPhone? You learn something new every day.”
Hina’s face went red instantly. Yui laughed, delighted. “You hid it? That’s either really sweet or really dirty. I’m choosing both.”
“Didn’t want it popping up if someone grabbed the phone at school.” He pocketed it again, expression easy. “It was a good photo. Bad timing. I was already in the trees.”
Yui stepped closer, shameless. “Next time answer faster and maybe we won’t have to send proof. Or do. Your call.” She glanced at Hina, who was still covering her cheeks. “Food’s on the counter. Sit. Both of you. And Beardy—if you disappear into the forest again tonight after saving our tits, I’m telling Janice you volunteered for morning inventory.”
Curtis huffed a short laugh and headed for the food. “Noted. I’ll stay in.”
Hina finally dropped her hands, still pink, and followed. The three of them moved into the small kitchen while the hidden photo sat on his phone and the low, steady pull in his chest remained quiet under the ordinary noise of a late dinner.
ui watched him clear the last of the rice, arms crossed, grin turning pointed. “So, Beardy-kun. You know what we meant by sending that photo last night.”
Curtis set the empty container down and shrugged. “You were being nice and showing me something to motivate my training.”
Hina pinched the bridge of her nose. “No.”
Yui stared at him for a second, then laughed so hard she had to lean on the counter. “Motivate your training. He really said that.” She wiped her eye. “Curtis. We sent you our tits. Faces covered. At midnight. While you were in the murder woods. That was not a pep talk.”
He blinked, processing. The scarred hand rested on the counter. “Oh. You wanted me to come back.”
“We wanted you to come,” Yui said, shameless. Hina made a dying sound behind her hands. “Home. And then not leave again until morning. The photo was the invitation. You saved it and went back to punching trees.”
Curtis looked between them, the realization settling slow. “Huh. I did think it was generous.”
Hina groaned into her palms. Yui just grinned wider and pushed another container toward him. “Eat more. Then we’re explaining, slowly, what ‘come over’ means when it’s attached to a nude. And you’re not allowed to call it training motivation ever again.”
He picked the container up, expression even, the corner of his mouth twitching. “Alright. Explain.”
The kitchen stayed warm. The low, steady pull in his chest remained quiet while the two girls tried to walk him through a social cue he had completely missed and the hidden photo sat on his phone, no longer just a nice gesture.
Hina stayed behind her hands for another second, then dropped them. Her face was still hot. She watched Curtis take another bite like they were discussing train schedules, and the want that had been sitting under her skin since the photo went out sharpened into something frustrated and fond at the same time.
He saved it. He hid it. And he still thought we were just being nice.
Yui was still talking, explaining in blunt detail what the message had actually been. Curtis listened, nodding occasionally, the scarred hand loose on the counter. He wasn’t embarrassed. He wasn’t teasing. He was just… catching up. Hina’s chest ached with it.
We were soaked when we took that picture. I kept thinking about his mouth. About riding him again. And he went and trained until his lip split because he didn’t get it.
She stepped closer without thinking. Her fingers brushed his wrist, right over the scar. He glanced at her, easy, and didn’t pull away. The touch sent another wave of heat low in her stomach. Free Love didn’t care that he was dense. It just made her want to climb into his lap right there and make the invitation impossible to miss.
“We wanted you here,” she said, quieter than Yui. “With us. Not in the forest. The photo was… us saying that. Clearly. Or we thought it was.”
Curtis looked at her hand on his wrist, then at her face. Something shifted behind his eyes. Not full understanding. Just the start of it. “Okay. Next time I’ll ask what you mean instead of guessing.”
Hina’s thumb stroked the scar once. She didn’t move her hand. Please ask. Or don’t. Just stay. The thought was simple and hot and a little desperate. She kept it behind her teeth and stayed where she was, shoulder against his arm, while Yui kept talking and the kitchen held all three of them in the warm, unfinished explanation.
Curtis set the container down. He looked between them, the new understanding still settling, and asked it plain.
“So. Tonight?”
That was all they needed.
Free Love hit hard and fast. The leftover want from the photo, the hand-holding, the way he’d finally asked—it snapped. Both girls moved at once. Yui grabbed the front of his shirt and yanked him down into a kiss that was all teeth and heat. Hina pressed in from the side, mouth on his neck, hands already shoving the suspenders off his shoulders. They were flushed, breathing uneven, done with explanations.
Curtis let them. His hands found Hina’s waist and Yui’s hip, steadying, not stopping. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while they dragged him toward the couch, clothes coming loose, the invitation finally answered in the only language that didn’t need translating. The apartment door stayed locked. The forest could wait. Tonight he wasn’t leaving.
They didn’t make it to a bedroom.
Yui shoved him down onto the couch and climbed into his lap, kissing him hard while her hands worked his belt open. Hina was already stripping her own shirt off, then his, mouth trailing down his chest. Free Love had them both past talking. Curtis’s hands moved—palming Yui’s ass, then sliding up Hina’s back—meeting them instead of just receiving.
Yui got his pants down enough. She sank onto him with a broken moan, taking him deep in one roll of her hips. “Fuck—finally—” Hina straddled his thigh, grinding, kissing Yui over his shoulder, then his mouth when Yui tipped her head back. The three of them moved together, messy and urgent. Curtis thrust up into Yui, scarred hand gripping her hip, the other between Hina’s legs, fingers sliding through how wet she already was.
The couch creaked. Clothes ended up on the floor. Hina came first, shaking against his hand and Yui’s mouth. Yui followed, riding him through it until he spilled inside her with a low groan. They didn’t stop. Hina took his place next, sinking down while he was still hard, and Yui kissed her through the stretch. Curtis let them set the pace again, but he wasn’t passive this time. He met every roll, every kiss, the low, steady pull in his chest quiet under the sound of them finally getting what the photo had been asking for all along.
The night stayed locked outside. Inside, they kept going until the want burned down to something slower and the couch was a lost cause.
They ended up in Hina and Yui’s bed eventually. The couch was damp and crooked. No one cared. Curtis lay in the middle, Hina tucked against his left side, Yui against his right, all three of them loose and breathing even. The sheet was half on the floor. His scarred hand rested on Hina’s hip. Yui’s leg was thrown over his.
No one spoke for a while. The apartment was quiet except for the low hum of the fridge and the city outside the window.
Hina traced the line of the old scar on his palm. “You stayed.”
“You asked.” His voice was low, rough from earlier. “And I got it this time.”
Yui huffed a soft laugh against his shoulder. “Progress. Next time just come upstairs when we send the photo. Save us the explanation.”
“Noted.” He didn’t move to get up. The low, steady pull in his chest was calm. Free Love had done its work; neither girl felt the need to measure the other. They just stayed where they were, warm against him, while the forest and the Festival and the cut on his lip all waited somewhere outside the room.
Hina pressed a kiss to his chest. “Sleep. Janice will still yell if you show up tomorrow looking like you didn’t.”
“I will.” He closed his eyes. Yui’s arm tightened across his stomach. The three of them settled. The night kept moving beyond the window, and for once he wasn’t walking toward it.
